      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#1 · Aion chs. I–V (paraphrased from the consulted 1979 paperback; §/page locators in the house)",
          "title": "Ego, shadow, and the image of perfection",
          "italic": "The sequence demonstrates the psychology: an exclusively good and luminous figure does not include all the opposites active in the psyche — recognising an evil factor as real confers no goodness on evil acts.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The ego first appears as the centre of reference for conscious contents, rooted in bodily and psychic conditions exceeding what it knows of itself. The shadow requires moral effort because its traits belong to the person who repudiates them; projection makes an inwardly active factor seem a property conclusively encountered outside, and the world gives back the rejected face. The anima/animus drama deepens the problem of relation — the demand that another person embody an image prevents that person from being met; Jung's gendered scheme",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "stays in his historical account. The approach to the Self carries a double danger: overwhelm by the larger whole, or appropriation of it as one's own magnitude. Christ enters as a culturally effective symbol of the Self — the heavenly man, the second Adam — yet the perfection of the image produces the difficulty: an exclusively luminous figure does not include all the opposites. Antichrist belongs to the ensuing drama: the contrary excluded from the representation of totality remains active and acquires a compensatory historical figure.\n\nOccurrences: the shadow and projection (#1, recalled at the Shadow Quaternio #4); the double inflation danger (#1); the privatio boni argument (#1); the compensatory Antichrist (#1, #2).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
