      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#3 · Aion chs. X–XII (§§193–271)",
          "title": "The fish is caught, worked, and made to transform",
          "italic": "The work demonstrates the reversal: the practitioner works with the power that previously held the vessel — the substance's alteration returning as an alteration in the capacity to see.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "Alchemy alters the position of the human participant: the image becomes something to find and work upon, and the relation between worker and transforming substance becomes part of the transformation. Within the Turba tradition as Jung reads it, a round sea creature without bones or outer covering is heated slowly and treated with sea water until it shines; the preparation becomes an eyewash through which the practitioner can understand the art — the substance's alteration returns as an alteration in the capacity to see. Jung's jellyfish identification is an interpretation; a sixteenth-century adept reads an actual fish. The stone within the fish leads through Saturn, lead and the bright",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "mercurial substance concealed within darkness, toward the lapis; the filius philosophorum mediates the relation to the redeemer. The Carcassonne revelation's two fishes yoked beneath the earth keep their distinct narrative office from the Christian pair. The Echeneis brings the reversal into focus: the small fish that halts ships is now attracted by a magnet that can be taught — Mercurius naming substance and philosophy together. The aftermath changes the scale: the alchemical epoch gives birth to science and recedes as an outlived mother; by the eighteenth-century division of faith and knowledge, the investigating psyche disappears from the account of objective knowledge while making it possible.\n\nOccurrences: the heated creature and eyewash (#3); the Echeneis reversal and the taught magnet (#3); the two yoked fishes kept distinct (#3); the scientific aftermath and the forgotten condition (#3, #5→0).",
